\paragraph{Differential nocuity.}
Building on \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s nocuous/innocuous distinction, our results support a \emph{differential nocuity} hypothesis \citep{willis_nocuous_2008}: risk depends on type, safety training, and disambiguation channel availability. The binary effect is domain-robust (interaction $p = 0.187$) but type rankings are not ($\rho = 0.26$), suggesting nocuity is universal while its type-specific profile requires per-domain calibration. Nevertheless, 60.8\% of violation variance remains unexplained, reflecting conversation trajectory and user-simulator stochasticity; clause-level auditing addresses the largest controllable factor (22.4\%) but does not eliminate deployment-time randomness.

\paragraph{Taxonomy as diagnostic tool.}
Type membership explains only 0.4\% of violation variance versus 22.4\% for clause wording; however, it predicts failure \emph{mechanism} 17$\times$ more strongly than violation rate (NMI ratio $\approx 17$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}), validating the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool: each type maps to a dominant failure mechanism (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

